---
title: "Working with an agent"
description: "Driving the whole authoring loop from a program: the headless commands, the check gate, the MCP server, and why there is no write tool."
---

Taliesin is built so a *program* can drive the whole authoring loop with no
browser. That program is usually an AI coding agent, but the same commands serve
an editor's language features and a CI job. Every one of them is **offline** and
**read-only toward your source**: the `.tmd` is the single editing surface, and
these commands only read, render, and validate it. This page is the workflow; the
[CLI reference](../reference/cli.tmd) has the per-command flags.

## The headless loop

The loop an agent runs is the same one you run, minus the browser:

1. **Scaffold.** `taliesin new post my-post` (or `taliesin init` for a whole
   project) writes a document that is correct on its first save: it renders, and
   `check` passes on it with zero diagnostics. `--json` prints a `{kind, slug,
   created, preview}` receipt instead of a human hint.
2. **Edit the `.tmd`.** Directly, as text, the same file a human would open. The
   one rule is below: edit the source, never the preview.
3. **Check.** `taliesin check <path> --format json` renders in memory and prints an
   object with a `diagnostics` array and an `environment` section. A non-empty
   `diagnostics` array (and a non-zero exit code) means there is something to fix.
4. **Read back.** `taliesin read <file>` projects the rendered document to plain
   text, the agent's substitute for looking at the preview.

```sh
taliesin new post my-post --json      # 1. scaffold (check-clean on first save)
# 2. edit posts/my-post/index.tmd
taliesin check posts/my-post/index.tmd --format json   # 3. gate
taliesin read posts/my-post/index.tmd                  # 4. read back
```

## The `check` gate

`check --format json` is the machine-readable gate an agent drives in place of
opening a browser. Each diagnostic carries a stable `code` (for example
`TAL-XREF-UNREF`), a `severity`, the `file` and `line`, a `message`, a `docs_url`,
and, for a near-miss, a `suggestion` with the `replacement` to apply. It never
executes code cells, so it is fast and deterministic (runtime cell errors stay in
`preview` / `build`). To expand any code into its cause and canonical fix without a
file:

```sh
taliesin check --explain TAL-XREF-UNREF
```

The full catalogue of `TAL-*` codes lives in
[Troubleshooting](../reference/troubleshooting.tmd).

## Reading back what you made

`taliesin read` is what the agent "sees": headings, the resolved `Figure N` /
cross-reference numbers, callouts, fenced code, and math as raw TeX. It is static by
default, so add `--run` to also execute `{python}` / `{r}` cells and report what each
produced, and, when a local headless Chrome is available, whether each `{js}` chart
painted. That is how an agent confirms a computed figure actually baked, again with
no browser.

`taliesin map <dir>` is the whole-project outline in one call: every page in nav /
chapter order with its prose length and heading outline, the nav and any `mounts:`,
and the cross-reference graph (each anchor, where it is defined, and its backlinks).
It is how an agent plans an edit across a site without opening each file.

`taliesin skim <dir>` goes one layer deeper, projecting the whole book to what a
reader skims: numbered headings, each section's opening sentence, and the captions,
callout titles and theorem statements that stand on their own. `map` answers *what
is here*; `skim` answers *does it read*. It prints the author's raw sentence rather
than a summary, and annotates a missing opening instead of dropping the row, so it
can be used as evidence about a document's structure rather than an opinion of it.

## The MCP server

`taliesin mcp` exposes that same loop as an offline stdio **MCP server** (Model
Context Protocol), so an MCP host can drive check / read / symbols / map / vocab /
build as tools without shelling out. It has **no write, edit, or preview tool**, by
design: the `.tmd` stays your direct edit surface.

::: {.callout-warning}
## The MCP server is not a sandbox

The no-write guarantee is about the edit surface, not containment. Every tool reads
any path you hand it, and `build` writes HTML beside that path and runs the
document's code cells. Contain it with the host's own sandbox and working directory.
:::

## Discovering the dialect

An agent never has to guess what Taliesin accepts. Three read-only commands describe
the surface, each generated from the validator's own lists so it cannot drift from
what `check` enforces:

- `taliesin vocab` emits every closed-set construct (front-matter keys, cell options,
  callout and theorem kinds, div classes, cross-reference prefixes) as JSON.
- `taliesin schema` emits the JSON Schema for front matter and `_site.yml` (the same
  schemas that power [editor autocomplete](../reference/configuration.tmd)).
- `taliesin symbols <file>` lists a document's headings, figures, and other
  cross-reference targets.

## The `AGENTS.md` onramp

`taliesin init` writes an `AGENTS.md` beside the project: a generated onramp that
orients an agent to exactly this loop, edit the `.tmd` and not the preview, gate on
`check --format json`, read back with `read`, discover the surface with `vocab` /
`schema` / `symbols`, plus the dialect's closed sets (callout kinds, cell options,
cross-reference prefixes, front-matter keys) and a worked figure-from-a-CSV recipe.
Because it is generated from the live validator vocabulary, it cannot drift from what
`check` enforces. Drop an agent into a Taliesin project and this file tells it how to
work with no explaining from you.

## Why there is no write tool

Every machine command reads, renders, and validates; none of them writes your source.
The agent edits the `.tmd` the same way you would, then validates and reads back, and
the preview, the MCP server, and the LSP server all stay read-only views. This is the
same single-editing-surface rule that keeps click-to-source unambiguous: there is one
writer, the source file, and one bridge back from the view, navigation, never a
write. An agent that respects it composes cleanly with you editing the same file.
